Every command below runs from this directory: the ones in a subshell step into `backend/` and come
back. `uvx fastro@{{ fastro.version }}` fetches the tool that generated the project, so a checkout
needs nothing installed first.

## Get it running

```bash
uv sync --all-extras --project backend
cp backend/.env.example backend/.env
uvx fastro@{{ fastro.version }} env secret          # put the key it prints in backend/.env
uvx fastro@{{ fastro.version }} make deploy local   # writes the compose files
docker compose up -d
```

That stack runs the API{% if has("taskiq") %}, the worker and the scheduler{% endif %} and the data stores it needs, with `backend/src` mounted so a change reloads. Outside production the app builds the schema from the models as it boots, so there is nothing to migrate yet: the API is on <http://localhost:8000>, and its docs on `/docs`.
{%- if has("accounts") %}
Create the first superuser from the `ADMIN_*` variables in `backend/.env`:

```bash
docker compose exec api python -m scripts.setup_initial_data
```
{%- endif %}
{%- if has("admin") %}

## The admin panel

It ships switched off. Set `ADMIN_ENABLED=true` in `backend/.env`, with `ADMIN_USERNAME` and
`ADMIN_PASSWORD` values of your own, and it is served at `/admin`. Without both credentials it
stays unmounted, whatever the flag says.
{%- endif %}

## Migrations

When alembic should own the schema instead of the boot-time `create_all`, write the first revision
from the models and start the database empty, because the one that booted already holds the whole
schema and an upgrade against it fails on tables that exist:

```bash
uvx fastro@{{ fastro.version }} db baseline
docker compose down -v
```

Set `CREATE_TABLES_ON_STARTUP=false` in `backend/.env`, then bring the stack back: a container
reads that file as it starts, so an edit needs `up -d` again.

```bash
docker compose up -d
docker compose exec api alembic upgrade head
```

`db baseline` starts a throwaway Postgres of its own, so it runs here rather than in the container,
and leaves the revision in `backend/migrations/versions/` to commit. To keep what is already in the
development database instead of dropping it, run `docker compose exec api alembic stamp head`: the
schema is there, and stamping records it as applied.

## Running the app on your machine

The stack publishes Postgres{% if has("accounts") or has("cache") or has("taskiq") %} and Redis{% endif %} on localhost, so the app can run outside it. Point `POSTGRES_SERVER` and every `*_HOST` in `backend/.env` at `localhost`, then:

```bash
docker compose stop api{% if has("taskiq") %} worker scheduler{% endif %}
docker compose up -d postgres{% if has("accounts") or has("cache") or has("taskiq") %} redis{% endif %}
(cd backend && uv run --no-sync uvicorn src.interfaces.main:app --reload)
```

The first line frees the port the stack publishes; without it nothing can bind it.
{%- if has("taskiq") %}

The worker and the scheduler run the same way, against the broker `TASKIQ_BROKER_TYPE` names:

```bash
(cd backend && uv run --no-sync taskiq worker src.infrastructure.taskiq.worker:default_broker)
(cd backend && uv run --no-sync taskiq scheduler src.infrastructure.taskiq.scheduler:scheduler)
```
{%- endif %}

## Work on it

```bash
(cd backend && uv run --no-sync ruff check . && uv run --no-sync ruff format --check .)
(cd backend && uv run --no-sync mypy src scripts migrations tests)
(cd backend && uv run --no-sync pytest)
```

`AGENTS.md` says where code goes and what not to edit. From this directory,
`uvx fastro@{{ fastro.version }} check` reads the project the way CI does, and
`uvx fastro@{{ fastro.version }} sync --check` tells you whether `src/wiring/` matches
`fastro.toml`.

## Change what it carries

```bash
uvx fastro@{{ fastro.version }} features            # what is selected, and what else there is
uvx fastro@{{ fastro.version }} add <feature>
uvx fastro@{{ fastro.version }} remove <feature>
```

Either one rewrites `src/wiring/`, the dependencies and `.env.example`, and says what it changed.
